{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father was assassinated in 1152 and Raymond III inherited the county at the age of twelve. Because he was a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was raised in the court of Jerusalem and was closely tied to the royal family. His upbringing exposed him to the complex politics of the Crusader states and prepared him for the challenges of ruling a frontier county surrounded by Muslim powers.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III faced constant threats from Muslim rulers especially Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. He was known for his cautious diplomacy and often preferred negotiation over open warfare. He was a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and served as regent twice during the minority of Baldwin IV. His relationship with Baldwin IV was strained because Raymond believed the king was unfit to rule due to his leprosy. Raymond also clashed with other nobles including the Knights Templar and the powerful Patriarch of Jerusalem. He was accused of being too independent and sometimes even suspected of collusion with Muslim enemies. Despite these accusations he remained loyal to the Christian cause and defended his lands with determination.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a decisive role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against the rash campaign led by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond argued that the army should avoid the desert and instead secure water sources. His advice was ignored and the Crusader army was crushed at Hattin. After the defeat Raymond retreated to Tripoli and tried to organize resistance. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era of cautious leadership in the Crusader states. Raymond III left behind a legacy of pragmatism and political skill in a time of escalating violence. His rule is remembered as one of the last attempts to balance diplomacy with defense in the face of overwhelming Muslim pressure.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.001701099332422018}, {"token": " H